window.KIT_INDEX=[{"u": "README.html", "t": "GitHub Copilot training", "s": "", "x": "Trainings in handing real file work to GitHub Copilot, the assistant inside Visual Studio Code (VS Code), and checking what it did. Each one works at your own pace, whether or not you attend a live session. New to the assistant? Start with Getting started."}, {"u": "README.html#the-trainings", "t": "GitHub Copilot training", "s": "The trainings", "x": "Getting started : your first hour with the assistant. Start here if you are new. Skills, in more depth : coming later. Agents : coming later."}, {"u": "README.html#help-for-every-training", "t": "GitHub Copilot training", "s": "Help for every training", "x": "Stuck on a step: When you are stuck . Something went wrong: If something goes wrong . Look something up: Questions , Glossary , Your organization's skills . Understand why: Why this assistant is different ."}, {"u": "README.html#reading-these-trainings", "t": "GitHub Copilot training", "s": "Reading these trainings", "x": "In a web browser: in Windows File Explorer, open this folder, then read-in-browser , and double-click README.html . Every prompt there has a Copy button, and the side menu lists every page. In VS Code: click a page in the list on the left and press Ctrl+Shift+V to read it as a page. The read-in-browser pages open as code in VS Code; open them from File Explorer instead. On GitHub: the pages are already readable, and the links work. Blanks in a prompt, the parts in angle brackets such as <your folder> , are yours to replace, brackets included, before you press Enter."}, {"u": "README.html#for-presenters-and-maintainers", "t": "GitHub Copilot training", "s": "For presenters and maintainers", "x": "Presenters: the facilitator folder, for whoever runs a live session. Whoever changes or extends these trainings: maintaining/README.md ."}, {"u": "getting-started/index.html", "t": "Getting started with GitHub Copilot in VS Code", "s": "", "x": "In this training you hand real file work to GitHub Copilot, the assistant inside Visual Studio Code (VS Code), and check what it did against the files. If your work arrives as files, such as ticket and change exports, notes, spreadsheets, a folder someone sent you, or an application you support, it is for you. You do not need to write code. This is the first training in a series, for beginners. More advanced trainings follow it; see all the trainings ."}, {"u": "getting-started/index.html#what-you-will-be-able-to-do", "t": "Getting started with GitHub Copilot in VS Code", "s": "What you will be able to do", "x": "Open a folder in VS Code and have the assistant make something in it, allowing only what you asked for. Have it read a folder of your own work and write something from it, then check that against the files. Get a plain account of an application you do not own, with one claim checked in its files. Write a request of your own and use it on a real piece of your work. You need a Windows computer with VS Code and a GitHub Copilot licence; see Before you start ."}, {"u": "getting-started/index.html#the-path", "t": "Getting started with GitHub Copilot in VS Code", "s": "The path", "x": "Start here : about fifteen minutes. You end with a folder the assistant made on your computer, and a check you did yourself. Lesson: your own files : about thirty minutes. You end with a file the assistant wrote from your own work, checked, and a request you wrote yourself. Lesson: an application you do not own : about twenty-five minutes, plus two steps later. You need an application's address. You end with a plain account of what the application does, with one claim checked in its files. Your first real chore this week : on your own work. In the list of files in VS Code or on GitHub, the pages are in alphabetical order; follow the order above instead."}, {"u": "getting-started/index.html#how-this-training-works", "t": "Getting started with GitHub Copilot in VS Code", "s": "How this training works", "x": "You do it yourself, at your own pace. No instructor is needed, whether or not you went to a session. The path below takes about an hour and a quarter, in sittings as short as you like. The assistant is your helper. It lives in VS Code, which you open in the first step of Start here; keep it beside these pages. You type or paste every request yourself, because operating it is the skill you are learning. When you are stuck on a step, a message or a word, ask it: When you are stuck . You check what it gives you against the files, with your own eyes, before you use it. It is not the Copilot in your office suite; why this one is different ."}, {"u": "getting-started/index.html#before-you-start", "t": "Getting started with GitHub Copilot in VS Code", "s": "Before you start", "x": "A Windows computer with VS Code installed, and the assistant signed in with a licence. If it is not, see Getting set up . [YOUR ORGANIZATION: who to ask when setup fails] If there is no one named here, ask whoever sent you this training. Which files you may use. Until your organization tells you otherwise, use only files with no customer names, account numbers, personal details, passwords or system addresses. Most ticket and change exports have some of these: delete those columns first, or use a copy with made-up values. Not sure a file counts? Use the practice files in this training's sample-files folder, until you can ask. [YOUR ORGANIZATION: which classes of data may be used with the assistant] A box that says YOUR ORGANIZATION marks an answer your organization supplies. If one still shows its bracketed description and there is no one to ask, follow the rule beside it and carry on. Only three can stop you: getting VS Code and the assistant set up, your licence, and access to an application's code. These pages were checked against VS Code [YOUR ORGANIZATION: version] . If a button or menu has a different name on your screen, your screen is right; ask the assistant where it went."}, {"u": "getting-started/index.html#coming-back", "t": "Getting started with GitHub Copilot in VS Code", "s": "Coming back", "x": "Not sure where you stopped? Open VS Code with this training and ask the assistant: Ask the assistant: I am coming back to the Getting started training. Look at the getting-started folder in this training, the practice folder and my own folder. Which steps look done, and which should I do next? Do not change anything. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter."}, {"u": "getting-started/index.html#everything-in-this-training", "t": "Getting started with GitHub Copilot in VS Code", "s": "Everything in this training", "x": "Learn: Start here , Lesson: your own files , Lesson: an application you do not own , Your first real chore . In order, once. More practice: the recipes , worked examples of other kinds of file work. Fix a problem: If something goes wrong . Look something up: Questions , Glossary , Your organization's skills once your organization has filled it in. Understand why: Why this assistant is different , ten short ideas; best read after a lesson. Go further: all the trainings , including the more advanced ones that follow this one."}, {"u": "getting-started/start-here.html", "t": "Start here: your first fifteen minutes", "s": "", "x": "The first unit of the training. You end with a folder the assistant made on your computer, and a check you did yourself. Before you begin, see Before you start . Do these in order. Nothing here changes a file you already have."}, {"u": "getting-started/start-here.html#1-open-vs-code", "t": "Start here: your first fifteen minutes", "s": "1. Open VS Code", "x": "Press the Windows key, type code , and press Enter. A program called Visual Studio Code opens. Check: a window titled Visual Studio Code, usually showing a Welcome page. If nothing by that name appears, see Getting set up ."}, {"u": "getting-started/start-here.html#2-open-this-training-in-vs-code", "t": "Start here: your first fifteen minutes", "s": "2. Open this training in VS Code", "x": "If this training is already on your computer: menu File, then Open Folder. Choose the training's own folder, the one that contains the getting-started and read-in-browser folders (not either of those), and click Select Folder. Reading these pages in a browser? The browser's address bar shows where they are: the training's folder is the one that holds read-in-browser . If you were given its web address instead: on VS Code's Welcome page, click Clone Git Repository, paste the address, press Enter, and choose where to save it; your Documents folder is a good place. When VS Code asks whether to open it, click Open. [YOUR ORGANIZATION: the training's address] If VS Code says Git is not installed, asks you to sign in, or the copy fails, see Copying the training or an application fails . If it came as a zip file, see The training came as a zip file . A box may ask whether you trust the authors of the files. This training is plain text pages, so click the button that begins \"Yes, I trust\". Check: the list on the left of VS Code includes README.md and the getting-started folder. If there is no list on the left: menu View, then Explorer. To read these pages inside VS Code as pages rather than raw text: click README.md in the list, then press Ctrl+Shift+V."}, {"u": "getting-started/start-here.html#3-open-the-chat-and-choose-agent", "t": "Start here: your first fifteen minutes", "s": "3. Open the chat and choose Agent", "x": "Menu View, then Chat. A chat box opens, usually on the right. At its bottom is a small mode selector . It should say Agent; if not, click it and choose Agent. Agent means the assistant may do things, not only answer. Check: the selector reads Agent. If the chat asks you to sign in, or no chat appears, see Getting set up ."}, {"u": "getting-started/start-here.html#4-say-something", "t": "Start here: your first fifteen minutes", "s": "4. Say something", "x": "Type this into the chat box, or paste it, and press Enter: What can you do in this folder? Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Check: a reply arrives within a few seconds. What it says does not matter yet; that it answered does."}, {"u": "getting-started/start-here.html#5-ask-it-to-explain-the-next-step", "t": "Start here: your first fifteen minutes", "s": "5. Ask it to explain the next step", "x": "This training's pages are in the folder you opened, so the assistant can read them. From now on it is also your helper. Try it before you need it: Ask the assistant: Read getting-started/start-here.md in this folder. In plain words, what does step 6 ask me to do, and what will I see? Do not do it yet. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Check: compare its answer with step 6 below. If they differ, the page is right; tell it so. Its directions about VS Code are sometimes for a different version: your screen is the truth."}, {"u": "getting-started/start-here.html#6-have-it-do-one-real-thing", "t": "Start here: your first fifteen minutes", "s": "6. Have it do one real thing", "x": "Type or paste: Create a folder named practice inside this folder, and inside it a file named hello.txt that contains today's date. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Before the assistant runs a command on your computer, it asks you: a button appears in its reply. That question is the safeguard, so read it before you answer; see Reading a permission request just below. It may also create or edit files in the folders you opened without asking; when it does, the reply lists the changed file with Keep and Undo. Click Keep when the file is what you asked for; Undo removes the change. Here, whichever happens, it should only create one folder and one file. Check: on the left, a folder named practice appears; click it, then hello.txt, and it opens with today's date. Open Windows File Explorer and go to the training's folder: the practice folder is there, on your disk."}, {"u": "getting-started/start-here.html#reading-a-permission-request", "t": "Start here: your first fifteen minutes", "s": "Reading a permission request", "x": "The request has two buttons: Allow, and one beside it that declines (it may say Skip or Cancel). Declining is always safe: nothing runs, and the assistant waits for you. You do not need to understand the whole command. Look for three things: Which folder. Does it name only the folder you asked about? A folder you did not mention means decline. Danger words. Remove, Delete, del, rm, Move, mv, Rename. If you did not ask for something to be deleted, moved or renamed, decline. Too long to read? Decline it, then ask the question below. If its answer names only what you asked for, reply \"Go ahead\". It asks again; this time, allow it. Afterwards check the list on the left: its answer is a claim, not proof. Ask the assistant: In one sentence, which files will this create, change, move or delete? Name the folders. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. An example to decline: you asked for a new file, and the request reads Remove-Item -Recurse practice\\old . That deletes a folder you did not mention. Decline it. Practise: you asked the assistant to write summary.md in your folder team-notes . Allow or decline each of these? a. New-Item team-notes\\summary.md b. Move-Item team-notes\\*.docx archive\\ c. A long program that ends by writing C:\\Users\\you\\Documents\\team-notes\\summary.md Show the answer Answers: a, allow: it creates the file you asked for, in your folder. b, decline: it moves files, and you did not ask for a move. c, too long to read: decline, ask which files it will create, change, move or delete, and allow it when the answer is only summary.md in team-notes . While you are learning, do not choose any option that allows commands without asking."}, {"u": "getting-started/start-here.html#7-have-it-look-at-your-real-files-changing-nothing", "t": "Start here: your first fifteen minutes", "s": "7. Have it look at your real files, changing nothing", "x": "If your Downloads folder holds exports from work systems, use your Documents folder instead, and change the prompt to match. This request reads only the names and sizes of files, not what is in them. Type or paste: Look at my Downloads folder. Tell me how many files are in it, grouped by type, and name the five largest. Do not move, rename or change anything. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. If a permission request appears, read it as above, then click Allow. Check: open the same folder in Windows File Explorer, sort by size, and compare the largest file with what the assistant named. If they differ, say so: That is not the largest file. Look again. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter."}, {"u": "getting-started/start-here.html#you-have-done-the-thing", "t": "Start here: your first fifteen minutes", "s": "You have done the thing", "x": "You told an assistant what you wanted in plain words, watched it act on your computer, and checked its work against what you can see yourself. Everything else here is that same move on things that matter to you. Look back at step 7's request: it said which folder, what to find, and what not to do. In the next lesson you learn to write requests like that yourself. Next: Lesson: your own files ."}, {"u": "getting-started/lesson-your-files.html", "t": "Lesson: your own files", "s": "", "x": "Unit 2 of the path, after Start here . Your work may be tickets, exports, spreadsheets or notes, or an application's code; either way you start here. About thirty minutes. By the end the assistant will have read a folder of your real files and written you something from them, you will have checked it against the files, and you will have written a request of your own. Nothing in this lesson changes your files, except one small file you add and then delete yourself. The assistant only reads them and writes new files next to them. Stuck? Tell the assistant what you expected and what you see; the words are in When you are stuck . A word you do not know: Glossary , or ask the assistant."}, {"u": "getting-started/lesson-your-files.html#1-pick-a-real-folder", "t": "Lesson: your own files", "s": "1. Pick a real folder", "x": "Choose one folder on your computer that holds work you actually do. Any of these: a folder of meeting notes or scratch notes, as text or Word files; one spreadsheet a system exported for you, such as a list of tickets, incidents or changes, saved in its own folder; a folder someone sent you that you have not had time to read. If you have nothing like that to hand, make one: copy three or four documents you have written recently into a new folder. Plain text, markdown and CSV files are read directly. Word and Excel files usually work too; the assistant may ask permission to run a small program to read them. If a file cannot be read, save it as text or CSV and try again. Until your organization tells you otherwise, use only files with no customer names, account numbers, personal details, passwords or system addresses. For an export , delete those columns first, or use a copy with made-up values. Not sure your files are allowed? Use the practice files instead: made-up meeting notes, a ticket export and some change requests, in this training's sample-files folder. Copy that folder to your Documents folder in Windows File Explorer, and use the copy as your folder from here on. [YOUR ORGANIZATION: which classes of data may be used with the assistant] Check: open two of the files and look. None of those things is in them."}, {"u": "getting-started/lesson-your-files.html#2-put-it-next-to-this-one", "t": "Lesson: your own files", "s": "2. Put it next to this one", "x": "Menu File, then Add Folder to Workspace, then choose your folder. You now have two folders open, this training and yours, and both show in the list on the left. The training stays open so the assistant can keep reading these pages and help you with them. VS Code may reload its window when you add a folder, and the chat may clear; that is expected, and nothing in your files changed. When you close VS Code later, it asks whether to save your workspace configuration. Click Save, type a name you will recognise, such as copilot-training , and save it in your Documents folder. Next time: menu File, then Open Recent, and pick that name to get both folders back. Check: the list on the left shows two folders, this training and yours, by name. If yours does not appear, ask the assistant, using the words in When you are stuck ."}, {"u": "getting-started/lesson-your-files.html#3-ask-a-question-that-changes-nothing", "t": "Lesson: your own files", "s": "3. Ask a question that changes nothing", "x": "From here on, prompts have blanks: parts in angle brackets, such as <your folder> . Replace each one, brackets included, with your own before you press Enter. Use the folder's name as it appears in the list on the left. With two folders open, always name the one you mean. Look at the files in <your folder> . Tell me what is there, how many of each kind, and what they seem to be about. Do not change anything. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Check: pick one number in the reply, such as how many Word files there are, and count them yourself in the list on the left. They should match."}, {"u": "getting-started/lesson-your-files.html#4-ask-for-something-written", "t": "Lesson: your own files", "s": "4. Ask for something written", "x": "If your folder holds notes, first add one file of your own to it, so you have an answer you already know. In the list on the left, right-click your folder, choose New File, type known.txt and press Enter. In the file that opens, type one sentence you made up, such as \"Decision: the team review moves to Thursday.\" Then press Ctrl+S to save it. A dot on the file's tab means it is not saved yet. If your folder holds a spreadsheet instead, do not make known.txt : note one row's category and date now. A CSV export works best; if the assistant asks to install something to read a spreadsheet file, decline and save the export as CSV instead. If your folder holds notes: Read every note in <your folder> . Write a file called summary.md in that folder with three sections: decisions made, open questions, and action items with owners. For every item, say which file it came from. Do not change the notes. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. If your folder holds an exported spreadsheet: Read <file name> in <your folder> . Tell me the columns and how many rows. Then write analysis.md in that folder with the top ten categories by count, the ten oldest open items with their age in days, and a one-paragraph summary. For each figure, say which column and filter you used. Do not change the export. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. If a permission request appears, read it as in Reading a permission request : it should name your folder and the new file only. If none appears, the file may have been written directly; go on to the check. If it cannot read a file, or writes the file somewhere else, tell it what happened in the words from When you are stuck . Check: the new file appears inside your folder in the list on the left, not in the training folder. Click it. If it shows as text with symbols, press Ctrl+Shift+V to read it as a page."}, {"u": "getting-started/lesson-your-files.html#5-check-it", "t": "Lesson: your own files", "s": "5. Check it", "x": "A check where you already know the answer. Look for your made-up sentence from known.txt . A good result lists it and names known.txt . (For a spreadsheet: find the row you noted, and check its category is counted and its age is right.) If it is missing, or names another file, you have just seen why checking matters. When you are done, delete known.txt : right-click it in the list, then Delete. Then pick two more items, ones you would act on. For each, open the file it says the item came from and find the sentence or row. Open a Word or Excel file from File Explorer, in Word or Excel; in VS Code it does not show as a page. To mark what you found, click the text tab of the file it wrote, summary.md or analysis.md (not the preview ), type (checked) after the item, and press Ctrl+S. The assistant can tell you where to look; only the file can tell you whether it is right. What a good result looks like. Every item names the file it came from, and the ones you checked are really there. What a bad result looks like. An item with no file named, or one you cannot find in the file it names. Treat that item as a guess, and say so, putting in the item and the file: Write this yourself in the chat, filling in each part: The item <the item> does not appear in <the file it named> . Re-do the summary using only what the files say, and name the file for every item. If you cannot find the file it names, ask: Ask the assistant: Which file and which line did <the item> come from? Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Then open it yourself."}, {"u": "getting-started/lesson-your-files.html#6-see-how-your-request-was-built", "t": "Lesson: your own files", "s": "6. See how your request was built", "x": "The notes request in step 4 has five parts. Every request you can check has them: Which folder or file: <your folder> . What to read: every note. What to make, and where: a file called summary.md in that folder, with three sections. What not to do: do not change the notes. Show your sources: for every item, say which file it came from. For figures from a spreadsheet, say which column and filter each one used. Now find the five parts in the other request in step 4, the spreadsheet one, before you read on. Show the answer Check your answer: 1 is <file name> in <your folder> ; 2 is that one file, its columns and rows; 3 is analysis.md in that folder, with the top ten categories, the ten oldest items and a summary; 4 is do not change the export; 5 is say which column and filter each figure used. A request with all five is one you can check. When a result comes back wrong, the missing part is usually why. The first request in a chat needs all five. Follow-ups in the same chat, such as \"Split the action items by week\", can be short: they build on the first."}, {"u": "getting-started/lesson-your-files.html#7-write-a-request-of-your-own", "t": "Lesson: your own files", "s": "7. Write a request of your own", "x": "Ask for a second file from the same folder, one you would actually use: a list of deadlines, a note to your team, three things to raise at your next meeting, or whatever you would have asked a colleague to pull from these files. Write it yourself. This frame is only a start; a file name ending in .md , such as deadlines.md , works well: Write this yourself in the chat, filling in each part: Read <what to read> in <your folder> . Write <file name> in that folder with <what it should contain> . Do not change <what must stay as it is> . For every item, say which file it came from. Write your request in a new file first, so you cannot lose it: in the list on the left, right-click your folder, choose New File, name it my-requests.md , type your request in it and press Ctrl+S. Then ask the assistant to review it. Copy the line below into the chat box, and after it paste your request (select it in my-requests.md , press Ctrl+C, click at the end of the line in the chat box, press Ctrl+V). Then press Enter: Ask the assistant: Before you do anything, tell me which of these is missing or unclear in the request below, and do not carry it out: which folder, what to read, what to make and where, what not to do, show your sources. The request: Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Change your request in my-requests.md using what it said, and save it. Then copy it into the chat box on its own and press Enter. Check: the new file exists in your folder, and one item in it is really in the file it names. my-requests.md now holds a request you wrote that works: the start of your own collection."}, {"u": "getting-started/lesson-your-files.html#what-you-can-now-do", "t": "Lesson: your own files", "s": "What you can now do", "x": "You can point the assistant at a folder of your real work, have it read the lot, get something written from it, and check it. You can also write the request yourself: the five parts are in step 6. Two checks go with you to every chore : Know one answer in advance. Before you ask, note one item you know should be there, such as a decision, a row or a change, and see that it comes back. Check what you would act on. Choose the items you would act on, not random ones, and find each in the file it names. Why this works: the workbench , the assistant has hands and check the receipts . Next: Lesson: an application you do not own . You need an application's address for it. Without one, go straight to Your first real chore this week ."}, {"u": "getting-started/lesson-application.html", "t": "Lesson: an application you do not own", "s": "", "x": "Unit 3 of the path, after Lesson: your own files . About twenty-five minutes for steps 1 to 4 and 6; steps 5 and 7 are for later, when a real question comes up and the next day. You need the address of an application's code that you are allowed to open (step 1 says what that is), and the practice folder from Start here . No address and nobody to ask yet? Leave this lesson for now and go to Your first real chore this week . You end with a plain account of what the application does and who uses it, a set of pages about it, answers to the questions you actually have, and one important claim checked against the file it came from. You will not read code. You will read what the assistant writes about the code, and check that the file it points at says what it says. Stuck? Ask the assistant, as before. (In a live session, the presenter walks steps 1 to 4 with you; do 5 and 7 afterward.)"}, {"u": "getting-started/lesson-application.html#1-of-7-get-the-application-s-address", "t": "Lesson: an application you do not own", "s": "1 of 7. Get the application's address", "x": "The code for an application is kept in a repository : one folder of its source files and supporting files, held in a shared system where changes are tracked and reviewed. You need the address of one you are allowed to open; your organization decides which, usually your own team's. An address looks like a web link that ends in the application's name. [YOUR ORGANIZATION: which repositories a learner may open, and where to find the address] With no address yet, and only if your organization allows fetching from the public internet: any public sample application works; a common one is the Bank of Anthos sample at https://github.com/GoogleCloudPlatform/bank-of-anthos . Check that policy before you try."}, {"u": "getting-started/lesson-application.html#2-of-7-bring-it-onto-your-computer-and-check-two-things", "t": "Lesson: an application you do not own", "s": "2 of 7. Bring it onto your computer, and check two things", "x": "Put the address where the prompt says <address> : I need to inspect this application. Clone the repository at <address> into the practice folder so we can analyze it. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. \"Clone\" means make a local copy. A button appears inside the reply asking whether to allow the command . Read it as in Reading a permission request : it should only copy the application into practice . Then click Allow. You did not learn any command to do that; you asked, and the assistant operated the tool. If it fails, see Copying the training or an application fails . Check two things before going on: A new folder appears under practice in the list on the left, with files inside it. A simple question gets a reply, and the kinds of file it names (for example files ending .java or .py ) can be seen in that folder: What kinds of files are in this application? Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter."}, {"u": "getting-started/lesson-application.html#3-of-7-ask-what-it-is-and-make-it-cite-files", "t": "Lesson: an application you do not own", "s": "3 of 7. Ask what it is, and make it cite files", "x": "What does this application do, what business problem does it solve, and who uses it? You sometimes make mistakes, so cite the files that support each part of your answer. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Read the answer in its three parts: what it does, the business problem it solves, who uses it. Then do the one thing that makes this safe: Open the first file you cited and show me the lines that support the \"who uses it\" claim. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Check: click the file name in its reply, or find the file in the list on the left, and find the lines it quoted. You are not reading the code; you are checking that the words the assistant quoted are really there. \"Who uses it\" is often the assistant's own interpretation: no file says it in those words. If it cannot show lines, that is a finding, not a failure; step 6 shows how to tell the difference. If they are not, or if part of the answer had no file behind it, say so: That part is not supported by the file you named. Re-answer using only what the files say, and cite a file for every claim. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. If you do not know what to ask, ask this: Ask the assistant: What questions should I ask you to understand this application? Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. If the answer is disorganized or does not make sense to you, say exactly that, and it redoes the work: Ask the assistant: Organize your response. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Ask the assistant: That does not make sense to me; explain it. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. The answer gives you orientation. The repository gives you the evidence."}, {"u": "getting-started/lesson-application.html#4-of-7-generate-a-set-of-pages-about-the-application", "t": "Lesson: an application you do not own", "s": "4 of 7. Generate a set of pages about the application", "x": "Ask for a set of linked pages about the whole application: Write a set of pages in the practice folder about this application, starting with an index: what it does, its main parts, what it depends on and what depends on it, the business rules in the code, its data, how it handles errors, and which files a new owner should read first. Cite files on every page. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. If your organization provides a documentation generator , you can use it instead. [YOUR ORGANIZATION: the command to run the generator, and what it produces] It reads every file in the application, so it takes five to ten minutes, not seconds. While it runs, the assistant shows its list of steps and works through them; the moving indicator means it is still working. Start it and come back. When it finishes, it names the first page, usually called index. Click it. If it shows as text with symbols, press Ctrl+Shift+V. Check: the index opens, and each line in it opens a page. Open the overview page, and find one file it cites in the list on the left."}, {"u": "getting-started/lesson-application.html#5-of-7-explore-by-the-question-you-actually-have", "t": "Lesson: an application you do not own", "s": "5 of 7. Explore by the question you actually have", "x": "This is where the pages become useful to you: to bring someone new onto the application, to plan a change, to support an incident, to settle who owns what. Do not read everything. With the pages open, ask what you would have asked the application's engineer. One example: How does this application work end to end? Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Other things people ask: what it depends on, and what depends on it; logic built into the code that you would not guess from the outside; what could break if it changes; how it is configured and monitored. Pick the one closest to a decision in front of you this month and ask it in your own words. Add the part that makes it checkable: ask it to name the page or file for each point. Check: open the page or file the answer names. If it does not exist, or does not say that, tell the assistant. If the chat panel has grown to fill the screen, drag its top edge down to see the pages again."}, {"u": "getting-started/lesson-application.html#6-of-7-check-one-important-claim", "t": "Lesson: an application you do not own", "s": "6 of 7. Check one important claim", "x": "Pick one claim from the pages that would matter if it were wrong: a dependency, a business rule, who is affected when something fails. Ask, putting in the claim: Write this yourself in the chat, filling in each part: You say that <the claim> . Show me the file and the lines that support it. Open the file. There are three kinds of claim, and you can tell them apart: Found directly in the code : a setting, a name, a connection to another system. You can see it. Worked out from how the code calls other things : one part hands work to another, in an order. You can follow it. The tool's own interpretation : what it thinks the code is for. It should be labelled as interpretation. For example: \"the database address is set in the configuration file\" is found directly; you can see the setting. \"A payment goes to the ledger after the balance is checked\" is worked out; you can follow one part calling the next. \"This service exists to keep balances accurate\" is interpretation; no file says it in those words. Practise first. Decide which kind each of these is, then compare with the answers below them: a. \"The service retries a failed payment three times.\" b. \"Orders are saved before the confirmation email is sent.\" c. \"This is the most critical service in the application.\" d. \"It connects to a database called ledger-db.\" Show the answer Answers: a is found directly, a setting you can see; b is worked out, from one part calling the next; c is interpretation, no file says it; d is found directly, a name and a connection. If you cannot tell which kind your own claim is, ask: Ask the assistant: Is this claim written in the file, worked out from how parts call each other, or your interpretation? Show me the lines either way. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Then decide yourself, using the examples above. A claim with no file behind it is unsupported: treat it as a guess, and say so to the assistant. Check: the lines say what the claim says, and you can name which of the three kinds it is. Before you use anything from these pages for a change to production, a risk decision, or a question of ownership, apply your own judgment. The pages are a starting point, not the truth: you have checked one claim, and the rest stay unchecked until someone checks them."}, {"u": "getting-started/lesson-application.html#7-of-7-come-back-tomorrow", "t": "Lesson: an application you do not own", "s": "7 of 7. Come back tomorrow", "x": "Close VS Code. Open it again: menu File, then Open Recent, then the name you saved your folders under (or this training's folder). Everything is still there, including the application and its pages. Start a new chat with the plus button at the top of the chat panel. A new chat remembers nothing of yesterday's conversation, which is the point: the folder remembers, not the chat. In the new chat, ask it in your own words to read the overview page under practice and tell you the three things someone in your role should know first. Check: the new chat has none of yesterday's messages; the application and its pages are still in the list; and one of the three points can be found in the overview page. Read the pages when you want the whole picture; ask the chat when you want one answer. Both are there whenever you need them, and nothing you did yesterday was lost."}, {"u": "getting-started/lesson-application.html#what-you-can-now-do", "t": "Lesson: an application you do not own", "s": "What you can now do", "x": "Start with the repository. Ask one direct question for orientation. Generate the pages for a structured understanding. Explore them by the question or decision in front of you. Trace important findings back to the file they came from. Treat generated knowledge as a starting point to check, not automatic truth. When it is wrong, and sometimes it will be, say what is wrong and it redoes the work; checking it is the skill , not trusting it. Next: Your first real chore this week ."}, {"u": "getting-started/first-real-chore.html", "t": "Your first real chore this week", "s": "", "x": "The last unit of the path, on your own work. Best after Lesson: your own files . Before you close this page, write two lines at the top of my-requests.md in your own folder: the chore you will do, and the day. Put fifteen minutes in your calendar for that day. On the day: Open the recipe for that kind of chore first, from the list below, and read its \"Before you start\" or \"Before you export \". For a fresh export, remove the columns the data rule rules out before the assistant sees it. Write the request yourself in my-requests.md in your folder, with the five parts from Lesson: your own files , and save it. Copy the line below into the chat box, paste your request after it, and press Enter: Ask the assistant: Before you do anything, tell me which of these is missing or unclear in the request below, and do not carry it out: which folder, what to read, what to make and where, what not to do, show your sources. The request: Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Send it. Change your request in my-requests.md using what it said, save it, copy it into the chat box on its own, and press Enter. Allow only commands that name the folders and files your request named. Check two items against the files: one you knew in advance, and one you would act on. Compare your request with the recipe's: if it has a part yours lacked, add it next time. The recipe for each kind of chore: Tickets, exports or spreadsheets: this week's export; compare with Analyze an exported spreadsheet . A good result is a table you checked against the export yourself. Decisions, approvals or planning: the next change or document that lands on you; compare with Review a change request or Find what is missing from documentation . A good result points at the passage for every answer. An application you support: one question you would normally ask its owner, asked as in Lesson: an application, step 5 . A good result is one you checked in the file it names. A chore no recipe covers: Write your own request . Check: a week from now, your folder holds the file the assistant wrote, with the items you checked marked, and my-requests.md with the request you wrote. That is your record."}, {"u": "getting-started/first-real-chore.html#you-have-finished-the-path", "t": "Your first real chore this week", "s": "You have finished the path", "x": "You can now point the assistant at a folder of your own work, write a request it can carry out and you can check, allow only what you asked for, and check the result against the files. Three habits go with you: A request has five parts: which folder, what to read, what to make and where, what not to do, and show your sources. Know one answer in advance, and see that it comes back. Check what you would act on, in the file it names. Where next: the recipes , for practice with other kinds of file work; Your organization's skills once your organization has filled it in; the more advanced trainings ; and [YOUR ORGANIZATION: office hours or a follow-up session] ."}, {"u": "getting-started/how-to/index.html", "t": "Recipes", "s": "", "x": "More practice after the path. Each recipe is a worked example of one kind of file work, about ten to fifteen minutes. Try the ones closest to your own work, with your own files or the practice files. Each assumes you have done Start here . Kind of work Recipe You end with A spreadsheet or export from a system Analyze an exported spreadsheet Tables you checked, in analysis.md A folder of notes Turn a folder of notes into one summary summary.md , every item sourced Documents someone handed you Find what is missing from documentation gaps.md , questions to send back A change or request to approve Review a change request for go or no-go review.md : go, no-go or ask, per change A messy folder Organize a messy folder A sorted folder and moves.md Many files to change the same way Change many files at once Changed copies, originals untouched Standards you keep re-explaining Set house rules once An instructions file it follows every time A job you do every week Ask for a skill A skill file you run by name Anything else Write your own request Your own request, checked By kind of work: Supporting an application: Find what is missing , Change many files at once , and Lesson: an application you do not own . Tickets, exports and notes: Analyze an export , Summarize notes , Organize a folder . Managers and analysts: Review a change , Find what is missing , Summarize notes . Not sure which fits? Ask the assistant: Ask the assistant: Read the getting-started/how-to folder in the training. My chore is <describe it> . Which recipe fits, and what would I change in it? Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter."}, {"u": "getting-started/how-to/analyze-an-export.html", "t": "How to: analyze an exported spreadsheet", "s": "", "x": "You end with: tables that answer your question, checked by you, in analysis.md next to the export . Before you start: VS Code open with this training's folder, the chat in Agent mode . New to it? Do Start here first. If you have done the lessons, try writing the request yourself first, then compare it with the one below. About fifteen minutes. When: a ticket export, an incident list, a change calendar, anything a system will give you as a CSV or spreadsheet file. Today you probably do this by hand in a spreadsheet. Before you export: until your organization tells you otherwise, use only data with no customer names, account numbers, personal details, passwords or system addresses. Delete those columns first, or use a copy with made-up values. Not sure? Practise on this training's sample-files folder first. [YOUR ORGANIZATION: which classes of data may be used with the assistant] Export the data from the system to a file. Put the file in a folder. Add that folder to VS Code: menu File, then Add Folder to Workspace, then choose it. This training stays open beside it. Orient. CSV is the format it reads most reliably; a spreadsheet file may work, depending on what is installed, and if it does not, export as CSV instead: Read <your export file> in <your folder> . Tell me the columns and how many rows. Then give me the top ten categories by count and the trend by week. For each figure, say which column and filter you used. Do not change the export. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Ask the question you actually have, in your own words. For a ticket export, for example, \"Which assignment group has the oldest open items? Show the ten oldest with their age in days.\" For a change calendar, \"Which changes next week have no named owner?\" Write this yourself in the chat, filling in each part: <your question about the data> Ask for something you can keep and send: Write analysis.md next to the export, with the tables above and a one-paragraph summary. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Check: open the export in your spreadsheet program. Filter one category and compare the count with the assistant's. Take one figure it computed, such as an item's age, and work it out yourself from the data. If either differs, ask: How did you compute that? Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Next week: save the new export under a different name, for example export-week2.csv , put it in the same folder, and ask: Compare <this week's file> with <last week's file> . What changed in the top categories, and which of last week's ten oldest items are still open? Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Direct access instead of an export: whether the assistant can read the system itself depends on connectors your organization has set up. [YOUR ORGANIZATION: available connectors and their status] If it does not work: If something goes wrong ."}, {"u": "getting-started/how-to/summarize-a-folder-of-notes.html", "t": "How to: turn a folder of notes into one summary", "s": "", "x": "You end with: summary.md , decisions, questions and actions from all your notes, every item naming the note it came from. Before you start: VS Code open with this training's folder, the chat in Agent mode . New to it? Do Start here first. If you have done the lessons, try writing the request yourself first, then compare it with the one below. About ten minutes. When: meeting notes, scratch files, exported chat threads, a week of scattered text. Add the folder to VS Code: menu File, then Add Folder to Workspace, then choose it. This training stays open beside it. Ask. With two folders open, the assistant needs to be told which one: Read every note in <your folder> , including Word files. Write summary.md in <your folder> with three sections: decisions made, open questions, and action items with owners. For every item, say which file it came from. Do not change the notes. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Open summary.md . If it shows as text with symbols, press Ctrl+Shift+V. Iterate in words: Split the action items by week. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Drop anything older than <a month> . Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Check: every item names a source file. Open the files for two items and find them there. An item with no source is a guess. If it does not work: If something goes wrong ."}, {"u": "getting-started/how-to/find-what-is-missing-from-documentation.html", "t": "How to: find what is missing from documentation you were handed", "s": "", "x": "You end with: gaps.md , the things the documents do not say, written as questions to send back. Before you start: VS Code open with this training's folder, the chat in Agent mode . New to it? Do Start here first. If you have done the lessons, try writing the request yourself first, then compare it with the one below. About fifteen minutes. When: an application team has sent you their documentation, an engineering spec, an export from the wiki, a game plan for a release or an event, and you have to support or approve something on the strength of it. You need to know what it does not say before you need it at two in the morning. Save the documents into one folder. If they live in a wiki, export or copy them to files first; the assistant reads files. Write a second short file, what-support-needs.md , listing what you need to find in any application's documentation: who to call, what it depends on, how to tell it is healthy, how to restart it, known failure modes, whatever your team's list is. No list yet? Ask the assistant to draft one for your kind of work, then cut and add until it is your team's. Add the folder to VS Code: menu File, then Add Folder to Workspace. Ask: Read every document in <your folder> . For each item in what-support-needs.md, say where in the documents it is answered, quoting the passage, or write \"not found\". Do not fill gaps from general knowledge. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Ask for the list you will send back: Write gaps.md in <your folder> : the items marked not found, each written as a question I can send to the application team. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. If the documents disagree with each other, ask it to list the contradictions with both passages quoted. Check: open one item marked \"answered\" and read the quoted passage in the source. Open one marked \"not found\" and search the documents yourself for the obvious word; if you find it, tell the assistant it missed one and ask it to look again. Why this works: the assistant is good at reading a lot of text against a short list. It is not good at knowing what your team needs; that is what the list is for, and it is worth writing once and keeping. If it does not work: If something goes wrong ."}, {"u": "getting-started/how-to/review-a-change-for-go-or-no-go.html", "t": "How to: review a change request for go or no-go", "s": "", "x": "You end with: review.md , one row per change: go, no-go, or ask, and why, with your decision still yours. Before you start: VS Code open with this training's folder, the chat in Agent mode . New to it? Do Start here first. If you have done the lessons, try writing the request yourself first, then compare it with the one below. About fifteen minutes. When: a change request has landed on you for approval, or you are the one who says go or no-go, and the answer depends on reading what was submitted against what you require. Before you export : until your organization tells you otherwise, use only records with no customer names, account numbers, personal details, passwords or system addresses. Delete those columns first, or use a copy with made-up values. Not sure? Practise on this training's sample-files folder first. [YOUR ORGANIZATION: which classes of data may be used with the assistant] Export the change record, or the list of changes awaiting you, to a file. Put it in a folder with your checklist: the things a change must have before you approve it, such as a rollback plan, a test result, a change window, a named owner, whatever your team requires. If the checklist is in your head, write it down first as a short file called checklist.md . Add the folder to VS Code: menu File, then Add Folder to Workspace. Ask: Read <your export file> in <your folder> and checklist.md. For each change, say which checklist items are met, which are missing, and which you cannot tell from the record. Do not guess; if the record does not say, write \"not stated\". Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Ask the question you would put to the submitter: For the changes missing a rollback plan, what would I need to ask the submitter? Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Keep it: Write review.md next to the export, one row per change: a suggested go, no-go, or ask, and why. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Check: open one change the assistant marked as fully met and confirm each item is really in the record. \"Not stated\" is the answer to trust; a confident \"met\" with nothing to point at is a guess. What this is not: the assistant does not approve anything. It reads the record against your list and shows you where to look. The decision is yours, as it was before. If it does not work: If something goes wrong ."}, {"u": "getting-started/how-to/organize-a-folder.html", "t": "How to: organize a messy folder", "s": "", "x": "You end with: a sorted folder, and moves.md , a record of every move. Before you start: VS Code open with this training's folder, the chat in Agent mode . New to it? Do Start here first. If you have done the lessons, try writing the request yourself first, then compare it with the one below. About ten minutes. The first time, try it on a small folder, or on a copy of one. When: your downloads folder, your desktop, a project folder that grew without a plan. A downloads folder can hold exports with customer data. The plan in step 2 reads only file names, but until your organization tells you otherwise, use a folder with no customer names, account numbers, personal details, passwords or system addresses in its files. Add the folder to VS Code: menu File, then Add Folder to Workspace, then choose it. This training stays open beside it. Adding a folder moves nothing. Ask for a plan before any action. With two folders open, the assistant needs to be told which one: Tell me how many files are in <your folder> . List them grouped by type and by month. Propose a folder structure. Do not move anything yet. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Read the plan. Change it in words, for example \"Put all the vendor PDFs under vendors/ instead\": <your change to the plan> Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Save the plan first, then move. The record exists before anything moves: Save the plan as moves.md in <your folder> : one row per file, where it is now and where it will go. Then move the files as moves.md says, and tell me any that did not move. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Before you allow each command , check it as in Reading a permission request : it should only move files inside your folder, as the plan said. Then allow it and watch the list on the left of VS Code change. Check: in File Explorer, right-click the folder and choose Properties. \"Contains\" shows the number of files, including those in the new sub-folders. It should equal the number from step 2, plus one for moves.md . Then open two files where they landed. If it went wrong: ask it to move the files back, and point it at moves.md , which records where everything was. That record is why step 4 saves it first. If it does not work: If something goes wrong ."}, {"u": "getting-started/how-to/change-many-files-at-once.html", "t": "How to: change many files at once", "s": "", "x": "You end with: changed copies of your files in a new folder, with the originals untouched. Before you start: VS Code open with this training's folder, the chat in Agent mode . New to it? Do Start here first. If you have done the lessons, try writing the request yourself first, then compare it with the one below. About ten minutes. When: a job you would never do by hand: rename two hundred files by a pattern, replace a term across a whole folder, pull every date or every address out of thirty notes into one table. Add the folder to VS Code: menu File, then Add Folder to Workspace, then choose it. This training stays open beside it. Ask for the outcome, and protect the originals. With two folders open, the assistant must be told which one, and this job changes files: Copy every file in <your folder> into a new folder called sanitized inside it, and in the copies replace every occurrence of <the old term> with <the new term> . Tell me how many files you copied and how many replacements you made. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. The assistant writes a small program, and a button appears in its reply asking permission to run it. You do not need to read the program: check the folder it names is sanitized , as in Reading a permission request . Then click Allow. It runs and reports. You asked for the outcome, not the program. The same shape, different job: Read every note in <your folder> . Make one table in <your folder> /dates.md of every date mentioned, the file it came from, and the sentence around it. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Check: in the list on the left, right-click the sanitized folder, choose Find in Folder, and type the old term: there should be no results. Then open one original file and confirm it still has the old term. If you want to see the program, ask for it; you do not need to: Show me what you ran. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Why this matters: you did not need to know how to code to take advantage of code. If it does not work: If something goes wrong ."}, {"u": "getting-started/how-to/set-house-rules.html", "t": "How to: set house rules once", "s": "", "x": "You end with: an instructions file in your folder that the assistant follows every time, without you restating it. Before you start: VS Code open with this training's folder, the chat in Agent mode . New to it? Do Start here first. If you have done the lessons, try writing the request yourself first, then compare it with the one below. About ten minutes. When: you keep re-explaining the same standards: your status categories, your fiscal year, terms you never use, the shape you want every summary to take. Add the folder where you do the work to VS Code: menu File, then Add Folder to Workspace, then choose it. This training stays open beside it. Ask, putting your own rules where the prompt says <your rules> . For example: our status categories are Red, Amber and Green; our fiscal year starts in October; never recommend a vendor by name; every summary ends with three next steps. Create the instructions file this assistant reads automatically, inside <your folder> , not the training folder. Put in it: <your rules> . Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. In VS Code with GitHub Copilot that file is .github/copilot-instructions.md . The assistant knows where it goes; you do not need to. Start a new chat , with the plus button at the top of the chat panel, so it cannot remember the rules from this conversation. Copy any recent document of yours into the folder, a status note or a summary. Ask for something from it without mentioning the rules: Write a three-line status note from <your document> in <your folder> . Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Change a rule by editing the file, or by asking the assistant to change it. Check: open the instructions file and read your rules there. Then read the status note from step 3: it should follow them without being told, for example using your status categories and ending with next steps. If it does not, the rules are not being read; ask the assistant why. [YOUR ORGANIZATION: any standard instructions file your teams share, and where it lives] If it does not work: If something goes wrong ."}, {"u": "getting-started/how-to/ask-for-a-skill.html", "t": "How to: ask for a skill", "s": "", "x": "You end with: a skill file you can run by name whenever the job comes round again. Before you start: VS Code open with this training's folder, the chat in Agent mode . New to it? Do Start here first. If you have done the lessons, try writing the request yourself first, then compare it with the one below. About fifteen minutes. When: you do the same analysis or the same kind of write-up more than twice. Describe the job, not the steps, in one or two sentences. For example: \"I handle tickets for one application. Every week I need to find what the tickets have in common.\" <the job you repeat> Write me a skill that does this on <what it works on> in <your folder> , and save it where skills belong in <your folder> , not the training folder. Tell me its name and where you saved it. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Look for the file it names in the list on the left; a skill is a file you can open and read. Run it on real work. Ask for it by name in plain words: Use the <skill name> skill on <your file> in <your folder> . Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Or type a forward slash in the chat box and start typing the skill's name; a list appears. Correct it in words, for example: Too verbose. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Group by root cause, not by requester. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Always end with three recommendations. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. It updates the file. Run it again. Check: the result names the file it worked on. Run it a second time on the same file; if the two runs disagree, the skill's instructions are too loose. Say what you meant and it tightens them. Open the skill file and read it: it should say, in words, what you asked for. Why this works: a skill is a text file of instructions. The assistant already knows how the job is generally done. The file tells it how you want it done. [YOUR ORGANIZATION: where skills are stored, and where approved shared skills are published] If it does not work: If something goes wrong ."}, {"u": "getting-started/how-to/write-your-own-request.html", "t": "How to: write your own request", "s": "", "x": "You end with: a chore done that no recipe covers, from a request you wrote yourself and checked. Before you start: VS Code open with this training's folder, the chat in Agent mode . Best after Lesson: your own files , which shows the five parts step by step. About fifteen minutes. When: a chore in front of you that none of the other recipes fits. Every request you can check has five parts. Most of the other recipes use all five: Which folder or file. Name it. With two folders open, always. What to read. Every note; one export ; the whole folder. What to make, and where. A file, by name, in which folder, with which sections or columns. What not to do. \"Do not change anything.\" \"Do not guess; if the files do not say, write not stated.\" \"Plan first; do not move anything yet.\" Show your sources. \"For every item, say which file it came from.\" Steps: Add the folder to VS Code if it is not open: menu File, then Add Folder to Workspace. Write your request in my-requests.md in your folder, with all five parts, and save it. Copy the line below into the chat box, paste your request after it, and press Enter: Ask the assistant: Before you do anything, tell me which of these is missing or unclear in the request below, and do not carry it out: which folder, what to read, what to make and where, what not to do, show your sources. The request: Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Change your request in my-requests.md using what it said, and save it. Copy it into the chat box on its own and press Enter. Allow only commands that match what you asked for; see Reading a permission request . my-requests.md keeps it for next time. Three examples, one for each kind of work: Tickets: \"Read incidents.csv in ops-exports . Write repeat-offenders.md in that folder listing every service with more than three incidents this month, with the count and the incident numbers. Do not change the export.\" Planning: \"Read every document in q3-planning . Write dates.md in that folder: every deadline mentioned, who owns it, and the file and sentence it came from. Do not change the documents.\" An application: \"Read the config folder of the application in practice . Write settings.md in practice listing every external system it connects to, with the file and line for each. Change nothing in the application.\" Check: the new file exists where you asked. Pick two items and find them in the files they name. If a result came back wrong, look for the missing part in your request; that is usually why. If it does not work: If something goes wrong ."}, {"u": "skills-in-depth/index.html", "t": "Skills, in more depth", "s": "", "x": "[TO BE WRITTEN: what this training covers, who it is for, how long it takes, and when it is offered] Finish Getting started first. This training builds on Your organization's skills and the recipe Ask for a skill ."}, {"u": "agents/index.html", "t": "Agents", "s": "", "x": "[TO BE WRITTEN: what this training covers, who it is for, how long it takes, and when it is offered] Finish Getting started first."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html", "t": "If something goes wrong", "s": "", "x": "Find what happened below, grouped by when it happens. Most answers end with words to give the assistant; it can read the files in the folders you have open, including this training, and it is usually the fastest help."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#when-you-are-stuck-ask-the-assistant-first", "t": "If something goes wrong", "s": "When you are stuck, ask the assistant first", "x": "Tell it what you expected and what you see. These words work for most problems. A step you do not understand: Ask the assistant: I am on step <number> of <page name> in the training folder. In plain words, what does it ask me to do, and what should I see? Do not do it for me. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. A step that did not work: Ask the assistant: I asked you to <what you asked> . Instead, <what happened> . What went wrong, and what should I try? Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. A message or error: Ask the assistant: I see this: <paste the message> . What does it mean, and what is the one thing I should do next? Do not change anything. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Something you cannot find on the screen: Ask the assistant: I cannot find <the thing> in VS Code. Tell me where it is, one step at a time. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. What it just did. Then check its list against the files yourself: Ask the assistant: List every file you created, changed, moved or deleted in this chat. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. A permission request you cannot read: Ask the assistant: In one sentence, which files will this create, change, move or delete? Name the folders. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. A word: Ask the assistant: What does <word> mean here, in plain words? Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Which recipe fits your chore : Ask the assistant: Read the getting-started/how-to folder in the training. My chore is <describe it> . Which recipe fits, and what would I change in it? Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Coming back after a break: Ask the assistant: I am coming back to the Getting started training. Look at the getting-started folder in this training, the practice folder and my own folder. Which steps look done, and which should I do next? Do not change anything. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Do what it suggests only once you have found on screen what it describes. It can be wrong about VS Code's menus and about what it did, so check against the screen and the files. It cannot tell you what your organization allows: whether a kind of data may be used, which applications you may open, your licence or sign-in. For those, and for more help, see the sections below."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#getting-set-up", "t": "If something goes wrong", "s": "Getting set up", "x": ""}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#vs-code-will-not-open-or-is-not-installed", "t": "If something goes wrong", "s": "VS Code will not open, or is not installed", "x": "If pressing the Windows key and typing code finds nothing, VS Code is not installed. You cannot go on without it. [YOUR ORGANIZATION: how to get VS Code and the assistant installed, and who to ask]"}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#the-chat-does-not-answer-or-copilot-is-not-responding", "t": "If something goes wrong", "s": "The chat does not answer, or Copilot is not responding", "x": "In order. Is the assistant installed: menu View, then Extensions, and type Copilot in the search box; it should show as installed. Is it signed in: the account icon at the bottom left of the window shows your name. Do you have a licence: in many organizations the assistant needs one to be granted to you before it will answer, and an unlicensed one fails silently or asks you to sign in again. Is the chat panel open: menu View, then Chat. If any of these fails, stop here and ask. [YOUR ORGANIZATION: how a licence is requested, and who to ask]"}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#it-answers-but-never-does-anything", "t": "If something goes wrong", "s": "It answers but never does anything", "x": "The mode selector at the bottom of the chat box is not on Agent. Click it and choose Agent. In the other modes the assistant only writes text; in Agent mode it can create files and run commands, after asking you."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#the-list-on-the-left-does-not-show-the-training-s-files", "t": "If something goes wrong", "s": "The list on the left does not show the training's files", "x": "If there is no list at all: menu View, then Explorer. If there is a list but not README.md and the getting-started folder, you opened a different folder: menu File, then Open Folder, and choose the training's folder again."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#the-training-came-as-a-zip-file", "t": "If something goes wrong", "s": "The training came as a zip file", "x": "Right-click it in Windows File Explorer, choose Extract All, and note where it went. Then open that folder in VS Code: menu File, then Open Folder."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#the-page-is-full-of-and-symbols", "t": "If something goes wrong", "s": "The page is full of # and * symbols", "x": "You are reading the raw text of a page in VS Code. Press Ctrl+Shift+V to read it as a page."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#copying-the-training-or-an-application-fails", "t": "If something goes wrong", "s": "Copying the training or an application fails", "x": "Three usual causes: the version-control program, called Git, is not installed; you are not signed in to the place the repository lives, called the code host; or network and proxy settings are not in place. If this happens while copying the training itself, the chat cannot help yet. Get the training as a zip file instead: on its web page, the green Code button, then Download ZIP; then see The training came as a zip file . Or ask the person who gave you the training. If it happens while copying an application, ask the assistant: Is git installed, am I signed in to the code host, and is a proxy configured? Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. It will check. [YOUR ORGANIZATION: how to install git, how to sign in to the code host, and the network setup]"}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#while-it-is-working", "t": "If something goes wrong", "s": "While it is working", "x": ""}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#i-pressed-enter-and-nothing-seems-to-happen-or-it-has-been-working-a-long-time", "t": "If something goes wrong", "s": "I pressed Enter and nothing seems to happen, or it has been working a long time", "x": "A moving indicator in the chat means it is still working. Some jobs take minutes: a set of pages about an application takes five to ten. If you want it to stop, use the stop control in the chat box, then say what you want instead."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#a-panel-full-of-text-opened-at-the-bottom-of-the-window", "t": "If something goes wrong", "s": "A panel full of text opened at the bottom of the window", "x": "That is where the commands it runs show their output. You do not need to type there. If the text worries you, copy it and ask the assistant what it means."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#it-asks-me-to-allow-something-and-i-do-not-understand-it", "t": "If something goes wrong", "s": "It asks me to allow something and I do not understand it", "x": "See Reading a permission request : which folder, danger words, and what to ask when it is too long to read. If in doubt, decline and ask: In one sentence, which files will this create, change, move or delete? Name the folders. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. While you are learning, do not choose any option that allows commands without asking."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#a-file-changed-and-i-was-not-asked", "t": "If something goes wrong", "s": "A file changed and I was not asked", "x": "The assistant may create or edit files in the folders you opened without asking; only commands it runs need your permission. When it edits a file, the reply lists it with Keep and Undo. Undo reverses that edit. It does not reverse commands it ran, such as moves and deletions."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#it-asks-me-a-question-back-or-offers-choices", "t": "If something goes wrong", "s": "It asks me a question back, or offers choices", "x": "Answer in plain words. If you are unsure, ask it which choice changes nothing."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#it-says-it-cannot-read-a-file", "t": "If something goes wrong", "s": "It says it cannot read a file", "x": "Plain text, markdown and CSV files it reads directly. For Word or Excel files it may need to write and run a small program to read them, and it will ask permission first. If that does not work in your setup, save the file as text or CSV and try again. Pages in a notebook application have to be exported to a file first."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#the-reply-is-too-long-too-technical-or-disorganized", "t": "If something goes wrong", "s": "The reply is too long, too technical, or disorganized", "x": "Say so. It redoes the work: Organize your response. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. That does not make sense to me; explain it in plain words. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#when-the-result-is-wrong-or-surprising", "t": "If something goes wrong", "s": "When the result is wrong or surprising", "x": ""}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#it-says-something-i-cannot-find-in-the-file-it-names", "t": "If something goes wrong", "s": "It says something I cannot find in the file it names", "x": "Treat that item as a guess, and say so: That does not appear in the file you named. Re-do it using only what the files say, and name the file for every item. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#it-did-something-i-did-not-ask-for", "t": "If something goes wrong", "s": "It did something I did not ask for", "x": "Stop it if it is still working. Then ask: List every file you created, changed, moved or deleted in this chat. Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Check that list against the list on the left or File Explorer, since its account of itself is not proof. Then ask it to put things back, using that list. If the chat offers to undo its edits to a file, that undoes edits only; it does not reverse commands it ran, such as moves and deletions. This is why the recipes that change files ask for a plan first and a record of what was done."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#it-worked-on-the-wrong-folder", "t": "If something goes wrong", "s": "It worked on the wrong folder", "x": "With two folders open, it has to be told which one. Name the folder in every request."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#it-has-forgotten-what-we-did", "t": "If something goes wrong", "s": "It has forgotten what we did", "x": "A new chat remembers nothing of the last one; the files do. Point it at the file: \"Read summary.md in <your folder> and continue from there.\""}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#the-conversation-has-gone-in-circles", "t": "If something goes wrong", "s": "The conversation has gone in circles", "x": "Start a new chat with the plus button at the top of the chat panel, and state the request again: which folder, what to read, what to make, what not to do, and to show its sources."}, {"u": "troubleshooting.html#when-to-ask-a-person", "t": "If something goes wrong", "s": "When to ask a person", "x": "The assistant cannot settle these, even if it answers confidently: installing or signing in, access to an application, and what data may be used. [YOUR ORGANIZATION: who to ask when setup fails] If there is no one named here, ask whoever sent you this training."}, {"u": "faq.html", "t": "Questions", "s": "", "x": "Something not working? See If something goes wrong ."}, {"u": "faq.html#about-this-training", "t": "Questions", "s": "About this training", "x": ""}, {"u": "faq.html#can-i-do-this-on-my-own-without-a-session", "t": "Questions", "s": "Can I do this on my own, without a session?", "x": "Yes. The whole path is written for that. Start at the README and follow the path in order; the assistant beside the pages is your helper when you are stuck."}, {"u": "faq.html#i-went-to-a-session-where-do-i-pick-up", "t": "Questions", "s": "I went to a session. Where do I pick up?", "x": "Every step is numbered. Open the lesson you were on and start at the step you reached. If you only watched, do the steps yourself from the start of that lesson; it goes quickly the second time."}, {"u": "faq.html#i-am-not-a-coder-what-am-i-supposed-to-do-with-this", "t": "Questions", "s": "I am not a coder. What am I supposed to do with this?", "x": "Use it on your own files. Nothing here requires you to write code, and the only reading of code is checking that a file the assistant cited contains the words it claims; you do not need to understand the code around them."}, {"u": "faq.html#is-this-the-same-as-the-copilot-in-my-office-suite", "t": "Questions", "s": "Is this the same as the Copilot in my office suite?", "x": "No. That one reaches across your organization's shared content: mail, chats, meetings, shared files. This one works on a folder of files on your computer and can act on them. They are for different things. Why this assistant is different explains in ten short ideas."}, {"u": "faq.html#your-data-and-your-files", "t": "Questions", "s": "Your data and your files", "x": ""}, {"u": "faq.html#does-what-i-type-or-the-files-it-reads-leave-my-computer-may-i-use-it-on-customer-data", "t": "Questions", "s": "Does what I type, or the files it reads, leave my computer? May I use it on customer data?", "x": "This is the first question to settle, and the answer is your organization's, not this training's. Until you have it, use only files with no customer names, account numbers, personal details, passwords or system addresses. For an export , delete those columns first, or use a copy with made-up values. Not sure a file counts? Use the practice files in this training's sample-files folder. [YOUR ORGANIZATION: what leaves the machine, what is retained, and which classes of data may be used with the assistant]"}, {"u": "faq.html#what-if-i-mess-up", "t": "Questions", "s": "What if I mess up?", "x": "On your own machine the assistant can change what you can change. It asks before it runs a command , unless you have allowed commands for the session, but it may create or edit files in the folders you opened without asking, and then offers Keep and Undo. A move, a rename or a deletion it makes cannot simply be undone, so for anything that changes files, ask for a plan first and for a written record of what it did; Organize a messy folder shows the shape. With two folders open, name the folder you mean in every request. In an application's repository , your copy is yours; nothing reaches the shared copy without review by other people. [YOUR ORGANIZATION: the review rule and process, by name]"}, {"u": "faq.html#what-it-can-work-on", "t": "Questions", "s": "What it can work on", "x": ""}, {"u": "faq.html#which-kinds-of-files-can-it-read", "t": "Questions", "s": "Which kinds of files can it read?", "x": "Text, markdown and CSV directly; Word and Excel usually, after asking to run a small program. See It says it cannot read a file ."}, {"u": "faq.html#can-it-reach-our-ticketing-system-logs-or-other-systems-directly", "t": "Questions", "s": "Can it reach our ticketing system, logs, or other systems directly?", "x": "That depends on connectors your organization has set up. Without one, export to a file and work on the file. That is Analyze an exported spreadsheet , and it is the same work you do by hand today. [YOUR ORGANIZATION: current connectors and their status]"}, {"u": "faq.html#can-i-open-any-application-s-code", "t": "Questions", "s": "Can I open any application's code?", "x": "Usually not; access is normally granted per application. [YOUR ORGANIZATION: how access is requested, and from whom]"}, {"u": "faq.html#can-i-download-skills-is-it-safe", "t": "Questions", "s": "Can I download skills? Is it safe?", "x": "A skill is a text file of instructions: read it before you use it, as you would any file someone sent you. See Your organization's skills for where approved ones are."}, {"u": "faq.html#does-disk-space-matter", "t": "Questions", "s": "Does disk space matter?", "x": "Delete a cloned application when you are done with it; you can clone it again. If size matters, ask the assistant how large the repository is before cloning. [YOUR ORGANIZATION: any policy]"}, {"u": "faq.html#getting-help", "t": "Questions", "s": "Getting help", "x": ""}, {"u": "faq.html#how-do-i-ask-it-for-help", "t": "Questions", "s": "How do I ask it for help?", "x": "Tell it what you expected and what you see, and ask it not to do the step for you. The words are in When you are stuck , and more are at the top of If something goes wrong ."}, {"u": "faq.html#how-do-i-move-the-chat-panel", "t": "Questions", "s": "How do I move the chat panel?", "x": "Layout is a personal setting, and everyone's screen differs. Ask the assistant: \"How do I move the chat panel to the right?\" It will tell you the setting or change it for you."}, {"u": "faq.html#your-organization", "t": "Questions", "s": "Your organization", "x": ""}, {"u": "faq.html#will-there-be-office-hours-or-a-follow-up-session", "t": "Questions", "s": "Will there be office hours or a follow-up session?", "x": "Ask whoever sent you this training. [YOUR ORGANIZATION: schedule and how to join]"}, {"u": "faq.html#can-i-suggest-what-to-cover-next", "t": "Questions", "s": "Can I suggest what to cover next?", "x": "Yes. [YOUR ORGANIZATION: where suggestions go]"}, {"u": "glossary.html", "t": "Glossary", "s": "", "x": "A word not here? Ask the assistant: \"What does <word> mean here, in plain words?\""}, {"u": "glossary.html#on-your-screen", "t": "Glossary", "s": "On your screen", "x": "Word Meaning here Agent mode The chat setting in which the assistant can act, not only answer. Chosen with the mode selector Chat The panel where you talk to the assistant Explorer, or the list on the left The panel showing the open folders and their files. If it is missing: menu View, then Explorer Mode selector The small selector at the bottom of the chat box. It should say Agent Permission request The button inside a reply that asks whether the assistant may run a command on your computer. How to read one: Reading a permission request Preview The readable view of a markdown page in VS Code. Ctrl+Shift+V opens it Terminal A panel at the bottom of the window where the commands the assistant runs show their output. You do not need to type there VS Code Visual Studio Code, the program the training runs in Workspace The folder, or folders, open in VS Code. The assistant reads and writes inside them. With two open, VS Code offers to save the pair under a name so you can reopen both"}, {"u": "glossary.html#working-with-the-assistant", "t": "Glossary", "s": "Working with the assistant", "x": "Word Meaning here Blank A part of a prompt in angle brackets, such as <your folder> , that you replace, brackets included, before pressing Enter Check the receipts Ask which files an answer came from, open one, and see whether it says that. An answer with no source is a guess Chore One piece of file work from start to finish: organize a folder, summarize a set of notes, analyze one export Command An instruction the assistant runs on your computer to do a job, such as creating a folder or moving files. It asks before running one Copilot GitHub Copilot, the assistant inside VS Code that this training is about. Not the assistant in your office suite CSV A plain-text spreadsheet export, and the format the assistant reads most reliably. Most systems that export to a spreadsheet can produce one Export A file a system gives you of its data, such as a list of tickets or changes House rules Your team's standards written once in an instructions file in your folder, so the assistant follows them without being told each time Markdown A plain-text format for documents. Raw, it shows # and * symbols; previewed, it reads like a page New chat A fresh conversation, started with the plus button at the top of the chat panel. It remembers nothing of earlier chats; the files do Prompt What you type or paste into the chat to ask for something Skill A text file of instructions that tells the assistant how to do a particular job your way Workbench The explanation page's word for VS Code with a folder open: a table you lay work out on and leave between sittings"}, {"u": "glossary.html#applications-and-code", "t": "Glossary", "s": "Applications and code", "x": "Word Meaning here Clone Make a local copy of a repository Code host The shared system where repositories live, with their history and reviews. GitHub is one Commit, pull request, merge The steps by which a change is proposed, reviewed and accepted into the shared copy Connector A configured link that lets the assistant read a system directly instead of an export Documentation generator A tool some organizations provide that writes a set of pages about an application from its code Extension An add-on to VS Code, installed from a marketplace, like an app on a phone. The assistant is one Git The version-control program that copies a repository to your computer and tracks changes to it. The assistant runs it for you Repository An application's source code and supporting files, kept in a shared system"}, {"u": "explanation.html", "t": "Why this assistant is different from the one you already have", "s": "", "x": "You already have an assistant in your office suite; in many organizations it is Microsoft 365 Copilot. It reaches across the content your organization shares: mail, chats and channels, meetings, files people have sent each other, your own cloud drive. It works inside those apps, and it is the right tool for that. This training does not replace it. The assistant in this training, GitHub Copilot inside Visual Studio Code, works on a folder of files on your machine, and it can act on them: create, move, rename, transform, run a small program, write a document, and leave all of that in the folder for next time. The two are not better and worse; they are for different things. Reach across shared content is the office assistant's strength. Acting on a folder of files you are working on is this one's. What this training is about, in three lines: Bring the assistant to your own work: your files, your exports, an application you support. It acts, and it asks first. Check what it says against the file it names. Two words to know. This page calls VS Code a workbench : think of a table you lay your work out on and leave it there between sittings. A folder of files sits open on it, and the assistant can read them and act on them. And a chore is one piece of file work from start to finish: organize a folder, summarize a set of notes, analyze one export . Below are the ten ideas the training rests on. Each names the idea, says what changes for you, gives one sentence to remember, and points at where in this kit you do it. They build in order."}, {"u": "explanation.html#1-it-meets-your-work-where-it-already-is", "t": "Why this assistant is different from the one you already have", "s": "1. It meets your work where it already is", "x": "Your work already lives in two places: the shared content your office assistant reaches, and the files you actually work on, such as downloads, an export a system produced for you, your notes, a folder someone sent you. This training is about the second place. Nothing here asks you to move your work into a new system. Put a folder of files on the workbench, from wherever they came, and the assistant comes to it. If a system you use every day has no direct connection yet, the export you already make is enough to start with. To remember: bring the assistant to your existing work, not your work to a new tool. In the kit: every recipe starts from a folder you already have."}, {"u": "explanation.html#2-the-workbench", "t": "Why this assistant is different from the one you already have", "s": "2. The workbench", "x": "VS Code is a workbench. Open a folder and everything in it is on the bench. The assistant can read those files, write new ones, rename, move and organize them. Come back tomorrow and the folder is still there, with what you and the assistant made. Work accumulates in one place, in files you own, instead of being scattered across conversations and pasted answers. To remember: a conversation produces answers; a workbench accumulates work. In the kit: Start here , step 6; Lesson: an application , step 7."}, {"u": "explanation.html#3-the-assistant-has-hands", "t": "Why this assistant is different from the one you already have", "s": "3. The assistant has hands", "x": "A conversation gives you advice and drafts. This assistant does the chore on the files in front of it. Ask for a folder and the folder appears. Ask it to sort thirty downloads into three folders by type and it does. Before it runs a command on your machine it asks first, unless you choose the option it offers to allow commands for the rest of the session. That question is the safety feature, not an obstacle. To remember: it does not draft advice; it does the chore. In the kit: Start here , steps 6 and 7; Organize a messy folder ."}, {"u": "explanation.html#4-you-direct-it-operates-the-tool", "t": "Why this assistant is different from the one you already have", "s": "4. You direct; it operates the tool", "x": "You do not learn the developer tool. You say what you need, in your words, and the assistant runs the tool: it fetches an application's files, it finds the setting, it opens the file. When you do not know how to do something in VS Code, ask the assistant. It either does it or tells you the one step to take. The tool is a means; the assistant is the operator. To remember: when you do not know how, ask it how. In the kit: Start here , step 5, asking it to explain a step; Lesson: an application , step 2, fetching by asking; When you are stuck ."}, {"u": "explanation.html#5-house-rules", "t": "Why this assistant is different from the one you already have", "s": "5. House rules", "x": "In conversation you tend to restate your team's standards each time you start one: the fiscal year, the status categories, the words you never use, the shape a summary should take. On the workbench you write them once, in an instructions file in the folder. The assistant reads it with every request you make there. Ask for a review of a document and the rules apply without you restating them. To remember: explain team standards once. In the kit: Set house rules once ."}, {"u": "explanation.html#6-skills-a-written-way-of-doing-one-job", "t": "Why this assistant is different from the one you already have", "s": "6. Skills: a written way of doing one job", "x": "The assistant knows a great deal in general. It does not know how your team wants a job done. A skill is a short instruction file that says how: how to write a status briefing, how to document an application, how to analyze a set of tickets. Your organization may provide some; see Your organization's skills . You can ask the assistant to write one, then correct it until it fits. Why skills exist: the assistant already knows how a status briefing is generally written. The skill says how your team writes one. To remember: general knowledge, plus your way of doing it. In the kit: Ask for a skill ; Your organization's skills ."}, {"u": "explanation.html#7-disposable-tools", "t": "Why this assistant is different from the one you already have", "s": "7. Disposable tools", "x": "A job you would never do by hand, across hundreds of files, usually waits for someone who can write a program. On the workbench the assistant is that someone: it can write a small program for a one-time job, run it, and throw it away: rename two hundred files by a pattern, replace a term across a whole folder, pull every date out of thirty notes into one table. You never see the program. You asked for the outcome, and it asked your permission before running anything. To remember: you do not need to know how to code to take advantage of code. In the kit: Change many files at once ."}, {"u": "explanation.html#8-the-loop", "t": "Why this assistant is different from the one you already have", "s": "8. The loop", "x": "A conversational answer is usually one pass. The assistant on the workbench can work a loop: do the step, check the result, fix what failed, continue. You give it the finish line and what \"done\" looks like; it iterates. You check a sample against the files, and everything it flags. To remember: state the finish line, not the steps. In the kit: Analyze an exported spreadsheet ; Turn a folder of notes into one summary ."}, {"u": "explanation.html#9-check-the-receipts", "t": "Why this assistant is different from the one you already have", "s": "9. Check the receipts", "x": "The assistant is sometimes confidently wrong. The habit that makes it safe to use is one move. Ask it to cite the files it used, and it names them. Open one. See whether the file says what the assistant said. An answer with no source is a guess. This is what you do with the output before you use it for a decision. To remember: an answer with no source is a guess. In the kit: Start here , step 7; Lesson: your own files , step 5; Lesson: an application , step 3; every recipe ends with a check."}, {"u": "explanation.html#10-where-a-repository-fits", "t": "Why this assistant is different from the one you already have", "s": "10. Where a repository fits", "x": "A repository is a folder holding an application's source code and supporting files, kept in a shared system. For an engineer it is where work happens. For everyone else it is a system you can ask questions of: what it does, what it depends on, who uses it, in plain language, with the files as evidence, and without waiting for an engineer to be free. In the lesson the assistant only reads; ask it to change nothing, and nothing reaches the shared copy without a review. And a repository describes what the application is meant to do, not what is running right now, so treat its answers as a first draft of the truth. Nothing new is needed for this: the same workbench, the same assistant, the same check. The folder is just bigger. To remember: an on-demand briefing on a system you do not own. In the kit: Lesson: an application you do not own ."}, {"u": "skills.html", "t": "Your organization's skills", "s": "", "x": "For someone who has done Start here . This page is a reference, not a lesson, and most of it is for your organization to fill in: come to it when you want to use a skill someone else made, find the ones your organization provides, or share one of your own."}, {"u": "skills.html#what-a-skill-is", "t": "Your organization's skills", "s": "What a skill is", "x": "A skill is a text file of instructions that tells the assistant how to do one job your way: how your team writes a status summary, how to analyze a set of tickets, how to document an application. The assistant already knows how such jobs are generally done. The skill says how they are done here. You can open a skill and read it; it is only text."}, {"u": "skills.html#how-to-use-a-skill-someone-else-made", "t": "Your organization's skills", "s": "How to use a skill someone else made", "x": "Two ways, either works: Ask for it by name in plain words, and name the file it should work on: Use the <skill name> skill on <your export file> in <your folder> . Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Type a forward slash in the chat box and start typing the skill's name. A list appears; pick from it. To see which skills are available to you right now, type a forward slash and read the list, or ask: What skills are available in this setup, and what does each one do? Copy Pasted it? Change the highlighted part in the chat box before you press Enter. Check: a skill's result should name the file it worked on. If it does not, or the result is not what the skill's description promised, say so and ask it to redo the job."}, {"u": "skills.html#where-your-organization-s-skills-are", "t": "Your organization's skills", "s": "Where your organization's skills are", "x": "[YOUR ORGANIZATION: where approved shared skills are published, how one is installed, and who maintains the list] Start with these. Each is one job; the name is what you say to use it. [YOUR ORGANIZATION: two or three skills to start with, each with a one-line job. For example: a skill that turns a ticket export into a weekly summary; a skill that documents an application from its code; a skill that drafts a monthly review from a folder of notes]"}, {"u": "skills.html#which-skills-to-expect", "t": "Your organization's skills", "s": "Which skills to expect", "x": "Skills follow the work people already do. Across an operations organization the same jobs recur on every team: reading incident and change exports, preparing a monthly review, documenting an application, analyzing failure modes, checking a runbook. Where your organization has a shared skill for one of these, use it rather than writing your own, so teams get the same answer the same way. [YOUR ORGANIZATION: the jobs your teams share, and which have a skill today]"}, {"u": "skills.html#asking-for-a-new-one-and-sharing-it", "t": "Your organization's skills", "s": "Asking for a new one, and sharing it", "x": "To have the assistant write you a skill for a job you repeat, see Ask for a skill . Once it works for you, others can use it. [YOUR ORGANIZATION: how a skill you made is shared, reviewed and published]"}, {"u": "skills.html#is-it-safe-to-download-a-skill", "t": "Your organization's skills", "s": "Is it safe to download a skill?", "x": "A skill is text. Read it before you use it, as you would any file someone sent you. [YOUR ORGANIZATION: whether skills from outside the organization may be used] Skills published inside your organization have already been through whatever review it applies."}, {"u": "skills.html#further-reading", "t": "Your organization's skills", "s": "Further reading", "x": "[YOUR ORGANIZATION: references on skills: the internal catalogue, the guide to writing one, and any research or comparisons the training team has assembled]"}];
